  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\n===== Tonight Through Tuesday =====\n\nKLVX and surrounding radars show light rain and/or drizzle \nprogressing northeastward across the area today, which is a result \nof a weak mid-level shortwave and associated vorticity. Lingering \ndry air in the lower levels has made it challenging for most \nlocations to receive beneficial rainfall. KY Mesonet obs show the \nhighest accumulations since midnight to be generally west of the I-\n165 corridor. \n\nExpect dreary weather to continue for tonight with light rain and \ndrizzle. With top-down saturation gradually taking place today, \nwe'll see our profile become more saturated by tonight as low \nstratus settles across the area. With a more favorable saturation \nprofile tonight, expect lingering light rain and drizzle to produce \nadditional accumulations through tomorrow morning. Between 18z today \nto 18z tomorrow, the best chances (70-80%) for 0.10\" of rain is \ngenerally west of I-75. For the Bluegrass region (mainly along I-75 \nand east), only a 40-50% chance for a meager 0.10\". \n\nRain will lift north of the area by Monday afternoon, leading to a \ndry period for the rest of the day on Monday through most of \nTuesday. This will occur as brief ridge builds downstream of a \ndeepening upper low over the Plains. There will likely be a temp \ngradient from north to south across the area tomorrow. Lingering \nrain and lower stratus will keep temps cooler north of the I-64 \ncorridor, with temps only reaching the low 60s tomorrow afternoon. \nHowever, drier and clearing clouds will help south-central KY warm \nto near 70. \n\nBy Tuesday, we'll still be in our brief dry period, though we will \nbe getting squeezed between a sfc high over the mid-Atlantic and the \ndeepening sfc low over the Central Plains. This will support a \ntightening sfc pressure gradient, and increasing SW flow will ramp \nup the WAA regime. Highs on Tuesday should reach the upper 70s, \nthough there is a non-zero chance for some areas to peak around 80. \nLEX could challenge their Nov 3 max T record, which currently stands \nat 77 in 2024. Winds could be gusty at times, and can't rule out \nsome gusts up to 25-30 mph. \n\n===== Tuesday Night - Saturday =====\n\nBy Tuesday night, a large upper level low will pivot a deep trough \naxis across the central US, driving a north-south oriented cold \nfront east of the Mississippi River by Wednesday morning. Ahead of \nthis front, we'll see rainfall activity arrive from the west Tuesday \nnight, and progress to the east through Wednesday morning. The LLJ \nahead of the sfc low will strengthen across the Ohio Valley Tuesday \nnight, potentially up to 55-60 kts, which will dramatically increase \nour moisture fetch into the area. PWATs could increase to 1.7-1.8\" \nby Wednesday morning due to the strong moisture transport, which \nwould be quite impressive given sounding climatology max is around \n1.6\" for BNA. Also, high shear is expected given the strong low \nlevel wind profiles, but fortunately little to no CAPE is realized \ndue to the nocturnal timing and very saturated profiles. Wednesday \nwill be a dreary, rainy day, but should see precip taper off from \nwest to east by Wednesday night. Drier weather is expected for \nThursday, but then a secondary wave will move through the area on \nFriday. We could see upwards to 1-2 inches of rainfall by the end of \nthe upcoming week, which would be beneficial given the ongoing \ndrought conditions.",
